---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'Odysseia';
const description =
  'A myth-inspired 3D adventure game developed by Gaucho Game Lab. I served as Puzzle Lead, designing and implementing systemic puzzle mechanics, levels, and gameplay systems in Unity.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>

      <!-- HERO -->
      <section class="hero">
        <img
          class="project-logo"
          src="/projects/odysseia/logo.png"
          alt="Odysseia logo"
        />

        <p class="eyebrow">
          GAME DEVELOPMENT · PUZZLE DESIGN · UNITY · C#
        </p>

        <h1>Odysseia</h1>

        <p class="lead">
          A myth-inspired 3D adventure game developed by Gaucho Game Lab.
          As <strong>Puzzle Lead</strong>, I designed, prototyped, and
          implemented systemic puzzle mechanics and mechanic-driven levels.
        </p>

        <div class="metadata">
          <div>
            <span>ROLE</span>
            <strong>Puzzle Lead</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>TEAM</span>
            <strong>15–20 Developers</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>DURATION</span>
            <strong>Sep 2024 – Mar 2025</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>TOOLS</span>
            <strong>Unity · C#</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- OVERVIEW -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">01</p>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            My work on <em>Odysseia</em> focused on building puzzle systems
            that could grow in complexity while remaining understandable to
            the player.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rather than designing isolated puzzle rooms, I developed reusable
            mechanics that could support multiple interactions, combinations,
            and level structures.
          </p>

          <p>
            I then designed levels around those systems and collaborated with
            other disciplines, particularly art, to improve visual
            communication, mechanic readability, and overall level polish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="contributions">
          <h3>My Contributions</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Designed and implemented a
              <strong>dynamic tidal system</strong> driven by the game's
              day–night cycle.
            </li>

            <li>
              Created a <strong>time acceleration mechanic</strong> that gives
              players direct control over tidal states.
            </li>

            <li>
              Designed a modular
              <strong>progressive light puzzle system</strong> supporting
              emission, redirection, dispersion, color fusion, and filtering.
            </li>

            <li>
              Designed multiple mechanic-driven levels using both the tidal and
              light systems.
            </li>

            <li>
              Conceptualized and prototyped the
              <strong>Minotaur's Labyrinth</strong>, using light dispersion to
              structure exploration and puzzle progression.
            </li>

            <li>
              Collaborated with the art team to improve mechanic readability,
              environmental communication, and level polish.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- TIDAL SYSTEM -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">02</p>
          <h2>Dynamic Tidal System</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            The tidal system turns the environment itself into part of the
            puzzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water levels rise and fall with the game's day–night cycle,
            dynamically changing which parts of the environment are
            traversable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/tide-high.png"
              alt="Odysseia environment during a higher tidal state"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Higher water levels cover obstacles and alter which routes are
              available to the player.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/tide-low.png"
              alt="Odysseia environment during a lower tidal state"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Lower tides expose terrain, passages, and interactable areas.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <p>
            During ebb tide, hidden spaces, objects, or routes can become
            accessible. During flood tide, rocks and reefs may become
            submerged, opening routes that would otherwise be blocked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The design goal was to move the player's reasoning beyond simply
            asking
            <strong>“Where should I go?”</strong>
            and introduce a second question:
            <strong>“When should I act?”</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- TIME ACCELERATION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">03</p>
          <h2>Giving the Player Control Over Time</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="media-text">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/time-speed.gif"
              alt="Time acceleration mechanic changing the environment and tidal state"
            />

            <figcaption>
              The player accelerates the day–night cycle to manipulate tidal
              states directly.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              A purely time-driven system introduces an obvious problem:
              waiting is not an interesting puzzle interaction.
            </p>

            <p>
              To address this, I implemented a
              <strong>Time Acceleration System</strong> that allows players to
              deliberately advance the day–night cycle.
            </p>

            <p>
              This gives the player meaningful agency over environmental state.
              Tides become something the player can plan around and manipulate
              rather than something they passively wait for.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- LIGHT SYSTEM -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">04</p>
          <h2>Progressive Light Puzzle System</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            I designed the light system around progressive complexity:
            introduce one understandable rule, then repeatedly expand what that
            rule can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            At its foundation, light travels from an emitter to a receiver.
            From that simple relationship, the mechanic expands into a larger
            system:
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mechanic-flow">
          <span>Emission</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>Redirection</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>Dispersion</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>Fusion</span>
          <b>→</b>
          <span>Filtering</span>
        </div>

        <figure class="wide-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/light-relay.png"
            alt="Light beam redirected between puzzle nodes"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Relay nodes expand the basic emitter–receiver interaction into a
            path-building puzzle.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <h3>From a Single Beam to a System</h3>

          <p>
            Relay nodes allow beams to be redirected across the environment.
            This transforms a simple activation mechanic into a routing problem
            in which players construct a path between multiple objects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prisms then split white light into red, green, and blue channels.
            This introduces branching logic, simultaneous signals, and
            dependencies between multiple parts of the puzzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            At higher levels of complexity, beams can be filtered or
            recombined. The system can therefore introduce new challenges
            without replacing the rules the player has already learned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/light-filter.png"
              alt="Light puzzle using a colored filtering surface"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Colored surfaces selectively filter channels, introducing
              constraint-based routing.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/light-complex.png"
              alt="Complex multi-room light routing puzzle"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Multiple beams, receivers, and filters allow puzzle logic to span
              larger environments.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- LABYRINTH -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">05</p>
          <h2>Minotaur's Labyrinth</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            The Minotaur's Labyrinth explores how a single puzzle system can
            shape the structure of an entire level rather than just an
            individual room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Traditional maze design creates a tension between exploration and
            guidance. A short solution path can cause players to skip large
            portions of the environment, while a long forced route can make the
            space feel overly linear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="wide-image concept-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/labyrinth-concept.jpg"
            alt="Early concept sketch of the Minotaur's Labyrinth showing red green and blue light routes"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Early concept sketch for the Minotaur's Labyrinth. RGB light paths
            were used to divide the maze into interconnected puzzle regions and
            structure player traversal.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <p>
            I used <strong>light dispersion</strong> as the structural backbone
            of the labyrinth.
          </p>

          <p>
            White light is divided into red, green, and blue routes, naturally
            separating the maze into three major regions. Each region contains
            a focused set of puzzles with enough depth to reward exploration
            without forcing the player into an excessively long linear path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cross-region interactions then reconnect these spaces. For example,
            a beam originating in one region can be required to activate or
            combine with a system in another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Persistent beams also become a form of environmental communication.
            Solved routes remain visible in the space, allowing the world
            itself to record player progress and hint at unexplored
            possibilities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION -->
      <section class="content-section technical-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">06</p>
          <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            The light puzzle system was implemented in Unity using C# with a
            modular, event-driven architecture.
          </p>

          <p>
            One of my main implementation goals was to separate
            <strong>light propagation</strong> from
            <strong>puzzle reactions</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="wide-image architecture-diagram">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/light-architecture.png"
            alt="Architecture diagram of the Odysseia light puzzle system"
          />

          <figcaption>
            System architecture showing how emitters, beams, receivers, and
            independent gameplay events communicate.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="technical-grid">
          <article>
            <p class="card-label">ARCHITECTURE</p>

            <h3>Event-Driven Modularity</h3>

            <p>
              Light receivers emit activation and deactivation events rather
              than directly controlling gameplay objects.
            </p>

            <p>
              Independent components can then respond to those events by
              opening doors, activating secondary beams, rotating objects, or
              triggering other gameplay behavior.
            </p>

            <p>
              This keeps the core light system independent from the specific
              objects that react to it.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <p class="card-label">EXTENSIBILITY</p>

            <h3>Specialized Receivers</h3>

            <p>
              A shared <code>LightReceiver</code> implementation handles common
              behavior such as light dependencies, color aggregation,
              activation state, and downstream propagation.
            </p>

            <p>
              Specialized receiver types extend that behavior only where
              necessary.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="code-concepts">
          <div>
            <code>LightReceiver</code>
            <p>
              Shared receiver logic for activation, incoming light, and
              propagation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <code>FilterLightReceiver</code>
            <p>
              Filters incoming color channels to support wavelength-based
              puzzle constraints.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <code>SpecificLightReceiver</code>
            <p>
              Activates only when incoming light matches the required target
              color.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- DESIGN PHILOSOPHY -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">07</p>
          <h2>Design Philosophy</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            Across both the tidal and light systems, my goal was to balance
            mechanic depth with mechanic variety.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful core mechanic should be simple enough for the player to
            understand, but flexible enough to support many different
            situations.
          </p>

          <p>
            The light system follows this principle by introducing complexity
            through combinations of familiar rules rather than continually
            replacing them with unrelated mechanics.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tidal system approaches the same idea differently: instead of
            increasing the number of objects, it changes the state of the
            environment itself and asks the player to reason about time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- TAKEAWAYS -->
      <section class="content-section final-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">08</p>
          <h2>What I Learned</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <em>Odysseia</em> changed how I think about puzzle mechanics.
            Systems become more useful when a small set of understandable rules
            can produce a wide range of interactions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The project also pushed me to think about game systems at multiple
            scales—from individual C# components, to puzzle rooms, to the
            navigation and structure of an entire level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Working as part of a larger development team also required me to
            communicate system behavior clearly and collaborate across
            disciplines so that gameplay logic, level design, and visual
            presentation supported the same player experience.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </main>

    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
